    89	### 7. Mobile and tablet
    90	
    91	This step is retained unchanged in the sequence. It may begin only after the approver approves desktop and the brief explicitly allows it. Until then its status is deferred, not passed, and no mobile/tablet layouts are made. For an approved desktop-only delivery, the brief must record that this step is outside the current scope; that record is not a mobile approval.
    92	
    93	When authorized, derive smaller layouts and navigation from the same copy, tasks and tokens. Document reflow and touch behavior without relying on hover. Test all key journeys and relevant states. The current rewrite does not authorize that phase.
    94	
    95	### 8. Validate, document and final design approval
    96	
    97	Freeze the complete scoped design: screens and applicable states, copy, tokens, assets, motion, semantic outline, SEO/schema intent, accessibility evidence and issue list. Run the integrated pre-handoff checker and the independent critic against this exact revision. Each result is PASS or FAIL with evidence; untested means FAIL for a required BLOCK check. A WARN includes its impact, owner and disposition and cannot hide an open implementation question.
    98	
    99	Validate WCAG 2.2 AA with automated and manual evidence. Include contrast pairs, keyboard and focus sequence, target sizes/exceptions, alt text, heading order, standalone action labels, dragging alternatives, authentication behavior where applicable, text resizing, reflow, RTL and screen-reader behavior. Automated axe and focus heuristics do not prove conformance. Resolve incomplete automated results through a documented manual check. One H1 per page is this package's audit contract, not a claim that WCAG universally mandates that count.
   100	
   101	The approver signs the final screen/state versions and hashes. A conditional design review may be recorded for history, but a pending claim, consent, asset, question or blocking result prevents Step 9. No AI reviewer supplies human approval.
   102	
   103	### 9. Handoff to the project's engineer
   104	
   105	Create the fixed package described in `references/handoff-contract.md`. Its canonical record is `handoff.json`; `HANDOFF.md` is the generated readable checklist. The designer, writer, engineer and QA gate verify the same criterion IDs and artifact hashes. Do not maintain competing lists with different acceptance rules.
   106	
   107	The package contains the private design link and exact frame names; DESIGN.md; project settings; DTCG-style token JSON and generated CSS with the actual build script/config and dependency lock; every screen's component/state inventory; versioned 1440-pixel PNG frames; a complete copy deck with slot placement screenshots; sourced/licensed assets; motion behavior; BLOCK/WARN checks; WCAG evidence; heading outline; image alt intent; standalone action labels; and page-specific SEO/schema intent. It also includes the critic record, tool evidence and the approver's signed record. Unknown schema eligibility is an open question, not invented structured data.
   108	
   109	Zero unresolved questions and zero placeholders are required. Each non-applicable state or audit criterion needs a reason and evidence, not an empty cell. The engineer acknowledges package completeness or identifies the exact missing criterion; Fannan fixes it before resubmission. Prototypes stay reference only. The engineer builds in the repository and stack in settings, the QA gate reports on the specific preview and commit SHA, and the approver follows the existing merge rule. A green PR or chat assertion is not a QA pass; missing proof fails, and a report on another SHA does not transfer.
   110	
   111	If any input fails, do not send a partial handoff. Record its owner and next action, set the task to Stuck or On hold and notify the approver through the already-authorized channel. Sending still requires the existing approval; this document does not send anything.
   112	
   113	## The screen-production loop inside the permitted step
   114	
   115	Keep the existing loop: **precheck → short plan and approval → Shape → Explore → Implement reference design → See → independent Critic → Harden → Review → design-share**. The plan states goal, permitted step, screens, checks and cost. Explore three to five private variants on one genuinely open dimension and have the approver choose; during wireframing the variants stay grayscale. Only the chosen direction advances. Do not generate options after a documented direction is already approved without an issue that reopens it.
   116	
   117	The critic is separate and read-only. It scores copy and design together using `references/critic-rubric.md` and records PASS/FAIL per check with a source or annotated frame. Internal zero-to-four scores track improvement; they cannot average away a failed hard rule. Stop after at most three rounds, or earlier when the total gains less than one point between rounds. On either stop, send the approver the scores, fixed defects, remaining severities and recommended next action. The cap never turns a failure into a pass and never permits a fourth round without a new authorized decision.
   118	
   119	## Folding in the approver's feedback
   120	
   121	Keep one feedback log per project, beside the issue list (`feedback-log`). Each item records an ID, the approver's exact words, date and channel, the screen/state/version it concerns, Fannan's interpretation, the decision (apply, clarify or conflict), the change made, the new version that carries it and the approver's confirmation. Ask a clarifying question when an item is ambiguous instead of guessing; an item that would break the WCAG 2.2 AA floor, a project rule, a confirmed fact or an approved earlier decision is reported as a conflict with the options, never applied silently. Re-present only after every item is resolved or explicitly deferred by the approver, and show what changed against the previous version. Feedback is scoped to the project and step where it was given. A preference that looks reusable is proposed to Fadel as a memory save; it is applied to other projects only after his written yes, and it never overrides a project's own rules.
   122	
   123	## Runtime, integrity and activation
   124	
   125	Keep the existing tool set and exact dependency versions. Choose the assignment's explicit **reviewed Node 22 upgrade** route for the original Node 20 host; the scripts reject unsupported runtimes rather than pretending compatibility. The proposed target is Node **22.23.3**, Linux x64, `node-v22.23.3-linux-x64.tar.xz`, SHA256 `df450af89261115ef9f9e3830c3eeb2cc9213b63c720b1af623cb5dcbe2e02de`; the versioned official checksum record is captured in `runtime/node-runtime.json`. Confirm target architecture and runtime compatibility, then obtain the existing Amin review and Fadel install approval before installing it alongside the old runtime. This Mac's Node 22.22.3 supports local tests, not proof of that upgrade. Install npm dependencies outside the manifest-covered release tree with the lockfile and `--ignore-scripts`. Every dependency consumer verifies the candidate's reviewed lock and installed-tree byte inventory before loading dependency code. An environment variable chooses the location, never the trusted bytes; changed target-platform bytes need a fresh inventory and Amin review before use. The complete skill/tool candidate, caller updates and rename map travel in one release with a new external `MANIFEST.sha256` and pin.
   126	
   127	Follow `references/security-and-install.md` for review, staging, backup, verification, atomic activation and rollback. All nine workflow skills and seven vendor skills are included. Historical old names remain only in the immutable source pack and the explicit migration map. The helper's existing filename and data path are not silently renamed. Unsupported media discovery/resume commands are removed from design-media rather than added to an unavailable helper.
   128	
   129	The four project roles are generic throughout the candidate. Proposed memory saves are supplied as a file; no live memory is updated. Save 8 contains the exact paid-action sentence above. The two deleted routines stay deleted; their promised verbatim archive is not in this pack, so its contents and preservation are untested.
   130	
   131	## Answers to the three latest questions
   132	
   133	**Matching avatars:** the rule is enforceable as an acceptance contract, not a guarantee from a generative model. Read the project-owned avatar registry first: each bot has one reserved animal identity and **no two bots share an animal**. Check the entire registered family for duplicates before generation and again before acceptance; a missing registry or duplicate assignment blocks the batch. Inspect the existing reference images, freeze their file hashes and define crop, camera angle, head scale, background, lighting, material/line treatment, palette and expression range. Use the same approved references and settings through the selected provider's available reference/style controls, and one approved anchor avatar for the batch. Record seed/model/version where the provider exposes them; a repeated seed is not a promise of identical style. Generate only after Fadel approves the specific paid job and any upload. Compare a contact sheet with references at equal size; reject inconsistent members rather than changing the style halfway through. No references were supplied here, so no avatar generation or matching test is claimed.
   134	
   135	**Gate before styling:** yes, make the existing Step 5 exit explicit. Run structural/copy checks on Step 4 grayscale output, fix failures, and obtain complete-set approval at Step 5 before Step 6. This strengthens the evidence at the existing boundary without changing the nine steps or adding an approval gate.
   136	
   137	**One Step 9 checklist:** yes. Maintain one canonical `handoff.json`, render its `HANDOFF.md`, and attach designer and QA results to the same versioned criteria. QA adds implementation evidence against the build SHA; it does not rewrite design acceptance. Any change to the package invalidates the affected evidence and approval until reviewed.
   138	
   139	## Threat model for the pre-handoff gate
   140	
   141	Pages are authored by Fannan or a human designer: honest but possibly sloppy, not an attacker. The gate must catch honest mistakes and ordinary authoring patterns completely (body or html clipping, zero-size or `display: contents` wrappers, open shadow roots, frames, objects and embeds, stylesheets loaded from `data:` or `blob:` URLs, state pages), and must fail closed on anything it cannot measure (closed shadow roots, unreadable or unserved stylesheets, frames, a vector field or native-function check that could not run, JavaScript-off content that is not equivalent).
   142	
   143	Deliberate obfuscation is outside the main threat model: for example JavaScript string escapes, comment tricks inside strings, patching `Function.prototype`, or base64-encoded stylesheets. Cheap defences are applied where they are simple (escapes are decoded and raw text is matched with and without comment stripping; the reduced-motion counter binds the native `requestAnimationFrame` at install and `call`/`apply`/`bind`/`Reflect.apply` are in the native-function check; `data:`/`blob:` stylesheets are refused). Anything beyond that is listed in `UNTESTED.md` as a known limit and is raised as ENH, not P0-P2, in future rounds. Defence in depth stays: computed-style checks, the JavaScript-off pass and the source scan.
   144	
   145	## Review convergence rule
   146	
   147	A review round passes when there are zero P0, P1 and P2 findings; P3 and ENH items are batched and don't block.
   148	
   149	## Assumptions and evidence boundaries
   150	
   151	Six type sizes, a strict ten-percent accent threshold and a 150-percent long-text stress case are design-policy choices made for this assignment, not scientific detectors of AI authorship. The Impeccable detector's findings are advisory; successful execution is not a design-quality PASS. Card-grid, generic-hero, dashboard-trope and decoration judgments require the scoped independent critic evidence in the same gate. English/Arabic filler and common three-part headline scans provide additional screening; semantic review in every project language remains mandatory. Passing these checks removes known bad patterns; it cannot establish that a human would endorse the result. The independent critic and approver remain essential.
   152	
   153	A **labelled example project** (the example fixture in the supplied review pack, `input/fannan-review-pack/example-project-*`) is used only as a dry-run of the workflow, in `references/example-project-check.md`. Its draft rules, absent authoritative brief/fact register and missing role/stack mappings are detected as blockers. Its project claims are not transplanted into this plan or promoted to confirmed facts.
   154	
   155	All local command results and negative-path checks are in the separate verification record. Anything not executed is in the untested list. Historical test claims stay historical. This candidate does not create Amin's review, live credentials, branch enforcement, provider access, a real approved design or a successful live handoff.

